/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74554" :question/id #uuid "d0050754-0064-5d7f-9d52-a3eaae555e94" :question/math-academy-id "q-74554" :question/problem "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-74554/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74554"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-74551/selection/3f73ed49abe2bf965d86692383c54dce6931611f202d34872769d6dad2caa508" :answer/id #uuid "96f0196d-eab9-5e71-b02b-479d4c267768" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},3,\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74551/selection/8de773d5883e8e5102d67675ddfdbaf63b9bbfe845153cada75ba4a855801039" :answer/id #uuid "ab5a388f-43c9-5ccc-9085-980c0e564a9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74551/selection/62fee8af8d95d14479445c89f7cbe2dede267b0c57d0cd677aa8ed2c7398cdbb" :answer/id #uuid "35190b1e-6c66-50d8-8706-647b767b38df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74551/selection/0a7e03708749034aa55b1dee27e1e57e62a6470d7f5399a54f46d1659f669f12" :answer/id #uuid "246e72be-cc71-592a-874c-e8cdba68e0a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},0,-\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74551/selection/7d9ba9c39ef08d00a7fdf35607725acaf543ba1d10e4f7acd7b7500ad8f30d8a" :answer/id #uuid "42f81c98-1976-5050-9629-8560de47f75a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},0,\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74551/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5dce7c85-0015-5e23-b3ca-75b6cf3883c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74551/selection/8de773d5883e8e5102d67675ddfdbaf63b9bbfe845153cada75ba4a855801039" :answer-field/choices ["q-74551/selection/3f73ed49abe2bf965d86692383c54dce6931611f202d34872769d6dad2caa508" "q-74551/selection/8de773d5883e8e5102d67675ddfdbaf63b9bbfe845153cada75ba4a855801039" "q-74551/selection/62fee8af8d95d14479445c89f7cbe2dede267b0c57d0cd677aa8ed2c7398cdbb" "q-74551/selection/0a7e03708749034aa55b1dee27e1e57e62a6470d7f5399a54f46d1659f669f12" "q-74551/selection/7d9ba9c39ef08d00a7fdf35607725acaf543ba1d10e4f7acd7b7500ad8f30d8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74551" :question/id #uuid "ea4a5d5e-f7f7-56bd-991c-22b5db64ad0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74551" :question/problem "The point $B$ has spherical coordinates $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,\\sqrt{3},3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-74551/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74551"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-74550/selection/870051f423a74a3b1efc1fd95bfbfcadd395d08ba0211ce64774020cacdebaab" :answer/id #uuid "6d55643c-58be-544f-ac93-eb3885a48693" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,-1,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74550/selection/0a13924286ef76728c60e5ee52241ef553fb794714a8cdd5fce5e66ebde494ab" :answer/id #uuid "fe465518-af88-50fb-a0ac-da057861cec1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74550/selection/e6f1fb94199f4b502538c42d1212124fc6a67b6307afa74bc00bceeb3bf54f00" :answer/id #uuid "8b846d6f-b6fc-5c11-96c6-6d73034cca97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,0,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74550/selection/345fc6b45792d9a853d4d26a7f88d1ca58b5b6daecd199747468377ff41d0b1a" :answer/id #uuid "cd1e01dd-ed33-58d5-8aa8-2af50db07734" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74550/selection/609840ba546ce11c95773031da5368f7e7d8cfbc5ab0ac8c5dc68d28e23a2034" :answer/id #uuid "e20963b8-ca2c-564e-8523-3bfef480c98f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,0,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74550/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b24941b-76b7-5d9f-8639-180d0ae1b4fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74550/selection/609840ba546ce11c95773031da5368f7e7d8cfbc5ab0ac8c5dc68d28e23a2034" :answer-field/choices ["q-74550/selection/870051f423a74a3b1efc1fd95bfbfcadd395d08ba0211ce64774020cacdebaab" "q-74550/selection/0a13924286ef76728c60e5ee52241ef553fb794714a8cdd5fce5e66ebde494ab" "q-74550/selection/e6f1fb94199f4b502538c42d1212124fc6a67b6307afa74bc00bceeb3bf54f00" "q-74550/selection/345fc6b45792d9a853d4d26a7f88d1ca58b5b6daecd199747468377ff41d0b1a" "q-74550/selection/609840ba546ce11c95773031da5368f7e7d8cfbc5ab0ac8c5dc68d28e23a2034"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74550" :question/id #uuid "e5768a91-c888-538c-93f2-c44301985e0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74550" :question/problem "The point $D$ has spherical coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $D.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $D$ are $(-1,0,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-74550/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74550"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-165297/selection/a8fe7beb5e9fd3d4c603beb5303a9217bf77e40292f027e81b704ac4166a856a" :answer/id #uuid "20ac82b6-7e80-5bdd-82fc-ac2763b575f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165297/selection/2e73eab472c6de0c25ff8a49945c707b14e33c7e6ea56131efd7bf438a80db65" :answer/id #uuid "2ea84e8a-6615-5b37-bbb0-41c46a77c690" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165297/selection/a604a0d96649ebfd51b573c1748e1c713313811f52786926d9b87bd5ad23fde4" :answer/id #uuid "5c532576-052c-54d6-894f-a8b076a54904" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165297/selection/f610780cad9981d8689e996259d014c4652d7da7c5fc57d0ee5c9137993b2a91" :answer/id #uuid "f7c182ad-48b0-5a5a-9373-6746255eb836" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165297/selection/94837346a47a8df880b8eca51b28386c00769f15ee78a11cde0a2c2b7731015b" :answer/id #uuid "3e6607f5-0190-5b4b-a365-040cd221d307" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165297/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "47cdb975-c902-50e4-a23b-e2e40accd173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-165297/selection/a604a0d96649ebfd51b573c1748e1c713313811f52786926d9b87bd5ad23fde4" :answer-field/choices ["q-165297/selection/a8fe7beb5e9fd3d4c603beb5303a9217bf77e40292f027e81b704ac4166a856a" "q-165297/selection/2e73eab472c6de0c25ff8a49945c707b14e33c7e6ea56131efd7bf438a80db65" "q-165297/selection/a604a0d96649ebfd51b573c1748e1c713313811f52786926d9b87bd5ad23fde4" "q-165297/selection/f610780cad9981d8689e996259d014c4652d7da7c5fc57d0ee5c9137993b2a91" "q-165297/selection/94837346a47a8df880b8eca51b28386c00769f15ee78a11cde0a2c2b7731015b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-165297" :question/id #uuid "613c94d2-7fac-5e2b-b2b7-18ae60e90291" :question/math-academy-id "q-165297" :question/problem "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-165297/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-165297"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-74555/selection/2bf0df7d6e4aa5cbe18fa52523dfb06683c4b2adec4e0446537833aeadd5e37c" :answer/id #uuid "df35ee61-64b2-5a87-8156-5b1f393b2033" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{3},-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74555/selection/b3a957d96ac7cbd5c8cf25f66dcc32f9a2e11f9d36b7f1af452cb0f323051b73" :answer/id #uuid "def9c837-70e9-5f5a-bcc9-275303f4b170" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74555/selection/dd6e196f148e2b2e2b2b931ef8b0b32ebeef1cab3587f3b546cc7b66e71fe642" :answer/id #uuid "abb65a18-8f76-5e5f-89bb-1f51f8391a82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74555/selection/862a0df7ef8253117c441355b6df0aaca081eb4326eba147d7f04aee16140ea7" :answer/id #uuid "7605f1ac-6fc2-5ac4-a345-b53f0596afd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74555/selection/d03424ebc794790f755a41444f7aa5ce014cc667ba368d78b89709def8dc219a" :answer/id #uuid "7175b52a-b645-541c-8141-b4b3e37f815e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74555/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b43aa9b6-add9-531f-8418-e0fa1d7bd699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74555/selection/b3a957d96ac7cbd5c8cf25f66dcc32f9a2e11f9d36b7f1af452cb0f323051b73" :answer-field/choices ["q-74555/selection/2bf0df7d6e4aa5cbe18fa52523dfb06683c4b2adec4e0446537833aeadd5e37c" "q-74555/selection/b3a957d96ac7cbd5c8cf25f66dcc32f9a2e11f9d36b7f1af452cb0f323051b73" "q-74555/selection/dd6e196f148e2b2e2b2b931ef8b0b32ebeef1cab3587f3b546cc7b66e71fe642" "q-74555/selection/862a0df7ef8253117c441355b6df0aaca081eb4326eba147d7f04aee16140ea7" "q-74555/selection/d03424ebc794790f755a41444f7aa5ce014cc667ba368d78b89709def8dc219a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74555" :question/id #uuid "9c504b49-7f27-5f56-866c-26d1ad54db4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74555" :question/problem "The point $P$ has spherical polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3},-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-74555/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74555"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-74725/selection/b8eaa506ea897fcac94fda8ee7c006c3d6a8f2e19fd5a11054f87c03d7b69d2d" :answer/id #uuid "9def0302-64ad-5360-b62d-2c6b71b2fe39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5,0.919,0.975)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74725/selection/a83cbbb53a2f08a6885b006636e49fc6a24a0f6b5dcebc4496e983816dc1c7cd" :answer/id #uuid "7151d119-50ac-5e2e-8870-d010546ce97f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{10},0.199,0.759)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74725/selection/8bf253a88e2d4384b8ecfed0839434924d8ee78e4733f7bf48b06656ac9ccb32" :answer/id #uuid "1d3b1ceb-33e9-5c43-a2fe-f9f83393deaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,0.991,0.957)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74725/selection/99c37dd09f636a6f249dad87e26bee6903969878a078027b1e38710d6a61adce" :answer/id #uuid "57052a14-d506-5281-ad93-d0b4607ce817" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(10,0.199,0.795)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74725/selection/671aa1d38a782b3237dcfd117b869eb3a2a8a5336ffd2e5886e9c6d96ddce922" :answer/id #uuid "90fa3a93-542f-5870-afac-f679c0175873" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0.991,6,0.975)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74725/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ac891ce-cd46-5f61-8a17-d015d223cb0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74725/selection/99c37dd09f636a6f249dad87e26bee6903969878a078027b1e38710d6a61adce" :answer-field/choices ["q-74725/selection/b8eaa506ea897fcac94fda8ee7c006c3d6a8f2e19fd5a11054f87c03d7b69d2d" "q-74725/selection/a83cbbb53a2f08a6885b006636e49fc6a24a0f6b5dcebc4496e983816dc1c7cd" "q-74725/selection/8bf253a88e2d4384b8ecfed0839434924d8ee78e4733f7bf48b06656ac9ccb32" "q-74725/selection/99c37dd09f636a6f249dad87e26bee6903969878a078027b1e38710d6a61adce" "q-74725/selection/671aa1d38a782b3237dcfd117b869eb3a2a8a5336ffd2e5886e9c6d96ddce922"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74725" :question/id #uuid "3cbb4f71-8dff-56c2-b60d-738177511ef4" :question/math-academy-id "q-74725" :question/problem "The point $S$ has Cartesian coordinates $(7,\\sqrt{2},7).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ of $S?$ Express $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+2+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(x,y)=(7,\\sqrt{2})$ lies in the first quadrant,  we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\  & ≈0.795.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $S$ are $(10,0.199,0.795).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74725/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74725"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-74341"] :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,-1,\\sqrt{2}).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-165299/selection/58c447779de138ab0396547514086d2a02bb461e1331045012e400d86138d96b" :answer/id #uuid "02fa50ce-38a5-56a2-b9af-540c9d648403" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165299/selection/d5e288bdad4c71a6a57294e428b27895f2f3227937270305438c1ddd7032876a" :answer/id #uuid "44055913-f6fd-5979-9b14-81f1813a4e69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165299/selection/563c915f814c7ff2bdb6a5565e823a1d5aad6c4621f55198c86e0b19fa1c5112" :answer/id #uuid "82954505-9375-5455-87de-2f1bcaa17553" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165299/selection/9280180a7842091e015e0e7ca9264e21d1e9f58c01ed5661315d4d1a55830c7d" :answer/id #uuid "9018945d-bc83-5c68-a522-4065274ba2ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165299/selection/e625aeb78aaef2de8071bff10f6abc2264a28bab3de1deb59d9f72f7f75a8587" :answer/id #uuid "2c49a66e-3402-5cc4-9fbf-97fb98c40cd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-165299/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0fde7d56-8839-5098-8c70-ac6f48c3ae9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-165299/selection/9280180a7842091e015e0e7ca9264e21d1e9f58c01ed5661315d4d1a55830c7d" :answer-field/choices ["q-165299/selection/58c447779de138ab0396547514086d2a02bb461e1331045012e400d86138d96b" "q-165299/selection/d5e288bdad4c71a6a57294e428b27895f2f3227937270305438c1ddd7032876a" "q-165299/selection/563c915f814c7ff2bdb6a5565e823a1d5aad6c4621f55198c86e0b19fa1c5112" "q-165299/selection/9280180a7842091e015e0e7ca9264e21d1e9f58c01ed5661315d4d1a55830c7d" "q-165299/selection/e625aeb78aaef2de8071bff10f6abc2264a28bab3de1deb59d9f72f7f75a8587"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-165299" :question/id #uuid "5090116f-465b-5350-9f1c-5fb94addf706" :question/math-academy-id "q-165299" :question/problem "The point $K$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $K?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$  lies in the third quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we add $\\pi $ to the reference angle to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={\\theta }_{R}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-165299/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-165299"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-74342"] :question/problem "The point $B$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,2,-\\sqrt{3}).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ of $B?$ Express $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(-1,2)$ lies in the second quadrant. So to find $\\theta $, we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{2}{-1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & ≈1.107\\,149\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -1.107\\,149 \\\\  & ≈2.034\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8}}) \\\\  & ≈2.230.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{5},2.034,2.230).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-74720/selection/b018b2d66f0235d213df1c42334a435fa796a2c737d13afc29e588863a844df8" :answer/id #uuid "67850a41-5022-547f-9055-c4e390ab1894" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74720/selection/1d93a4dfc247762d7f22906dc36cd887c80fe8e6546b4402f2bc2ab1bbee587c" :answer/id #uuid "98fb0622-7b79-57cc-bc99-813ef3db9e0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74720/selection/b2d7f917d4e8b8d4e010ffc6456ff0171567022a8a94ceb936d4b60aaf00a27e" :answer/id #uuid "e65a2972-0cac-5158-9c0a-4c5718670fe4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74720/selection/8721ea9a707211bb6de6ef9990d61a94f8148f66efdba21f43ca5467641fbb92" :answer/id #uuid "19b8583f-80c2-505e-946e-8f2d76d88ffb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74720/selection/621b718302da3ae906088fde06a8c5ec0f6d351f2a4e1f97f91beec45060681a" :answer/id #uuid "7d063f2b-ab57-5010-9c83-922134102066" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74720/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c6ab954-54de-59c2-8f1e-9029c3b4599d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74720/selection/1d93a4dfc247762d7f22906dc36cd887c80fe8e6546b4402f2bc2ab1bbee587c" :answer-field/choices ["q-74720/selection/b018b2d66f0235d213df1c42334a435fa796a2c737d13afc29e588863a844df8" "q-74720/selection/1d93a4dfc247762d7f22906dc36cd887c80fe8e6546b4402f2bc2ab1bbee587c" "q-74720/selection/b2d7f917d4e8b8d4e010ffc6456ff0171567022a8a94ceb936d4b60aaf00a27e" "q-74720/selection/8721ea9a707211bb6de6ef9990d61a94f8148f66efdba21f43ca5467641fbb92" "q-74720/selection/621b718302da3ae906088fde06a8c5ec0f6d351f2a4e1f97f91beec45060681a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74720" :question/id #uuid "df22d970-821c-535e-a8ba-c3e0c193b6b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-74720" :question/problem "The point $U$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,-\\sqrt{3},2).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(1,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $U$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74720/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74720"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6225"] :question/problem "What are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/02219461392f94791f20e3e7f0e125e846b602862524b381fa909530e0f4e9d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where $\\rho \\geq 0,$ $0 \\leq \\theta <   2\\pi,$ and $0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following: \n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(4, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6226"] :question/problem "The point $Q$ has spherical coordinates $\\left(2\\sqrt3, \\pi, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nCalculating $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-\\sqrt3, 0, -3).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6227"] :question/problem "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt2,0,\\sqrt2).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of  $P?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad\n\\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+0+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y) = (-\\sqrt2,0)$ lies on the negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, for $\\phi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(2, \\pi,  \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
